-- chem/inertiaq.cub — the inertia engine (chem/inertia.cub) for generalized problems H c = λ S c, rational matrices,
-- and certified brackets of a level. With S symmetric positive definite, S = LLᵀ, and H − λS = L(L⁻¹HL⁻ᵀ − λ)Lᵀ is
-- congruent to L⁻¹HL⁻ᵀ − λ, whose eigenvalues are the generalized eigenvalues shifted by λ; by Sylvester's law of
-- inertia the number of generalized eigenvalues below λ = p/q (q > 0) is the number of negative eigenvalues of
-- q·H − p·S, which Bareiss's leading minors and Jacobi's sign changes count (`iq-below`). For rational matrices
-- H = hn/dh and S = sn/ds (dh, ds > 0), q·H − p·S = ((q·ds)·hn − (p·dh)·sn)/(dh·ds), the same signs (`iq-below-rat`).
-- A bracket of level j (counting from 0, the lowest) is a pair of points lo < hi with at most j levels below lo and
-- at least j + 1 below hi, both counts read: then lo ≤ λ_j < hi (`iq-cert`). Bisection (`iq-bisect`) finds one on
-- dyadic points m/sc, sc a power of two, halving at each step; where a leading minor is zero at the midpoint (finitely
-- many points, the roots of the minors) it takes the point a quarter-step off it, and the bracket's certificate is
-- checked at its ends whatever the search did.
import "inertia.cub"

